<UserControl xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             xmlns:vm="using:Phi.Avalonia.Desktop2.ViewModels"
             x:Class="Phi.Avalonia.Desktop2.Views.ChatPageView"
             x:DataType="vm:ChatPageViewModel">
  <!--
    Chat transcript only. The header (top) and prompt input (bottom)
    were moved to ShellView.axaml so the ScrollViewer can wrap the
    whole right column at the window level — pinning them inside
    ChatPageView kept the ScrollViewer inside the 6* reading column
    so its scrollbar appeared mid-window, not at the window's right
    edge.

    ChatPageView is now just the centred transcript items control
    (the 2*,6*,2* column structure stays here so the transcript
    content itself stays aligned with the reading column, but the
    ScrollViewer that scrolls it lives one level up in ShellView).
  -->

  <!--
    Performance note: a plain StackPanel is used (not VirtualizingStackPanel)
    because the transcript is non-uniform (user bubble ~40 px, assistant
    markdown ~100-1000 px, tool card ~200 px). A virtualizing panel only
    realises items in/near the viewport, so on a session switch it can't
    know the total DesiredSize without measuring every item — its Extent
    is computed from the average size of the *realised* items, which for a
    200-item session where only the first ~40 are realised gives an
    estimate ~25-40% of the real extent. ScrollViewer.Offset then clamps
    to that bogus extent and the user lands in the middle of the transcript
    instead of at the bottom.

    The StackPanel measures every item once on the first layout pass, so
    the ScrollViewer's Extent is exact from the first frame and ScrollToEnd
    lands precisely on the last message.

    Cost: 200 chat-line UserControls are instantiated + measured on session
    swap. UserTextLineView is essentially free (one SelectableTextBlock);
    ToolCardLineView's body markdown only renders when the user expands
    the card. AssistantTextLineView is the expensive one (MarkdownViewer
    parses + lays out markdown) — that's the one session-switch latency
    pays for. A follow-up can add lazy markdown expansion (render plain
    text in Measure, swap to MarkdownViewer on first visibility) to bring
    session-switch latency back to virtualizing-panel levels without
    giving up scroll-to-end correctness.
  -->
  <Grid ColumnDefinitions="2*,6*,2*">
    <ItemsControl Grid.Column="1"
                  x:Name="TranscriptItems"
                  ItemsSource="{Binding Transcript}" />
  </Grid>
</UserControl>
